#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050240 :topic/id #uuid "38bc1722-543b-58cb-b448-fb93b0e623de" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073776 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073480 :question/id #uuid "f2105fec-c85b-5928-92e6-95ed80f1326c" :question/math-academy-id "e-14009" :question/problem "Show that the following system of equations has no solution.\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4x + 2y &=4 \\\\ 2x + y &=4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's start by solving the system using elimination.\n\nNotice that if we multiply the second equation by $-2$, then we will get a $-4x$ term that can cancel with the $4x$ term in the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4x+2y &=4 \\\\ 2x+y &=4 && \\text{multiply by } -2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhen we add the two equations, it turns out that both variables cancel, and we obtain a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4x + 2y &= 4 \\\\\n- 4x - 2y &= -8 \\\\\n0 &= (4 - 8) \\\\\n0 &= -4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe statement $0=-4$ is *always false*. Therefore, we conclude that the system has *no solutions*.\n\nGeometrically, this means that the corresponding lines *do not intersect*. In other words, they are *parallel.*\n\n![](images/04/048c377f99093f493c37bc4de0363527996c8928348b43ad9c9747965f984c1f.png)"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a24fb4f3-6e3c-5c45-b31e-85b7ce290eda" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Number of Solutions to a System"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073777 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073481 :question/id #uuid "b1d34db3-7f30-5a8c-b233-fd955ac656f0" :question/math-academy-id "e-14011" :question/problem "At how many points do the following lines intersect?\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx - 2y &= 2 \\\\[6pt]\n10y - 5x &= -10\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The number of points of intersection between the two lines is equal to the number of solutions of the corresponding system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x - 2y &=2 \\\\ 10y - 5x &=-10\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will solve the system using substitution. First, we solve for $x$ in the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx - 2y &= 2 \\\\\nx &= 2y + 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute\n\n$$\nx=2y+2\n$$\n\ninto the second equation and solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n10y - 5x &= -10 \\\\\n10y - 5(2y + 2) &= -10 \\\\\n10y - 10y - 10 &= -10 \\\\\n- 10 &= -10\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe result is a statement that's always true. Therefore, we conclude that the system has infinitely many solutions.\n\nConsequently, the lines intersect at infinitely many points (i.e., they coincide)."} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5fab4792-0fe1-5a5c-8754-509af469da68" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Number of Intersection Points of Two Lines"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073778 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073482 :question/id #uuid "ab09c3eb-6813-57c6-891b-bf20196cbe93" :question/math-academy-id "e-14014" :question/problem "Find all points of intersection of the following lines:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3x+2y &= 2 \\\\[6pt]\n6x+4y &= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The points of intersection of the lines correspond to the solutions of the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3x + 2y &=2 \\\\ 6x + 4y &=4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will solve the system using elimination. Notice that if we multiply the first equation by $-2$, then we will get a $-6x$ term that can cancel with the $6x$ term in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3x+2y &=2 && \\text{multiply by } -2 \\\\ 6x+4y &=4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhen we add the two equations, it turns out that both variables cancel, and we obtain a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-6x - 4y &= -4 \\\\\n6x + 4y &= 4 \\\\\n0 &= (-4 + 4) \\\\\n0 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause we obtained a true statement, we conclude that there are infinitely many solutions to the system. Therefore, the lines have infinitely many intersection points.\n\nWe want to find an expression that represents every solution to the system. Any value of $x$ is a solution, and we can find the corresponding $y$-coordinate for each $x$-coordinate by solving one of the lines for $y$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3x + 2y &= 2 \\\\\n2y &= 2 - 3x \\\\\ny &= \\frac{2 - 3x}{2} \\\\\ny &= 1 - \\frac{3}{2}x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are\n\n$$\n\\left(x,1-\\dfrac{3}{2}x \\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $x$ is any number."} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "50351666-c691-510f-863c-168c61f9980b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a System With Infinitely Many Intersection Points"}] :topic/math-academy-id 493 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048343} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049754} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050258} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050673}] :topic/title "Systems of Equations With No Solutions and Infinitely Many Solutions"}]}
